Offer reader mode when there is an article's worth of text.

The trained classifier said no to a zh.wikipedia article -- both its scores
negative -- and Chinese pages fare worst of all under it, for three structural
reasons. A paragraph has to reach 140 characters before it scores at all, and
that is counted in UTF-16 units, so the same content in Chinese is a third to
a half as many characters and ordinary paragraphs score zero. The URL features
count the percent-encoded path, so a four-character Chinese title arrives as a
thirty-six character slug studded with '%', which is unlike anything in the
training set. And the training set was English.

Moving to what Chrome for Android does would change nothing: Android runs this
same AdaBoost model for its simplified view, and on top of it declines on
mobile-friendly pages, which would make reader mode rarer here rather than
commoner.

So the model stops deciding on this platform and the question becomes the one
the product is asking: is there an article's worth of paragraph text? That is
mozScoreAllLinear, in characters, over <p> and <pre> outside list items. Six
hundred is roughly two Chinese paragraphs or one English one -- enough that an
index page or a search result does not qualify, low enough that a short news
piece does. The number is logged next to what the model would have said, so it
can be moved on evidence rather than argued about.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

diff --git a/components/dom_distiller/content/renderer/distillability_agent.cc b/components/dom_distiller/content/renderer/distillability_agent.cc
--- a/components/dom_distiller/content/renderer/distillability_agent.cc
+++ b/components/dom_distiller/content/renderer/distillability_agent.cc
@@ -6,7 +6,9 @@
 
 #include "base/json/json_writer.h"
 #include "base/metrics/histogram_functions.h"
+#include "base/logging.h"
 #include "base/strings/string_util.h"
+#include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "components/dom_distiller/content/common/mojom/distillability_service.mojom.h"
 #include "components/dom_distiller/core/distillable_page_detector.h"
 #include "components/dom_distiller/core/experiments.h"
@@ -173,6 +175,39 @@
   bool filtered = IsFiltered(parsed_url);
 
   bool is_distillable = distillable && is_long_article;
+
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // The trained model says no to pages the product wants to say yes to, and
+  // Chinese pages fare worst of all. Three reasons, all structural:
+  //
+  //  - a paragraph has to be 140 characters before it scores at all, counted
+  //    in UTF-16 units. The same content in Chinese is a third to a half as
+  //    many characters, so ordinary Chinese paragraphs score zero;
+  //  - the URL features count the percent-encoded path, so a four-character
+  //    Chinese title arrives as a thirty-six character slug studded with '%',
+  //    which is nothing the model saw while it was being trained;
+  //  - it was trained on an English corpus to begin with.
+  //
+  // Switching to what Chrome for Android does would change nothing -- Android
+  // runs this same model for its simplified view, and additionally declines on
+  // mobile-friendly pages, which would make it rarer here, not commoner.
+  //
+  // So the model does not decide on this platform. The question becomes the
+  // plain one the product is actually asking: is there an article's worth of
+  // paragraph text here? mozScoreAllLinear is that, in characters, summed over
+  // <p> and <pre> outside list items and saturating at 6000. An index or a
+  // search result has almost none; a short news piece clears the threshold in
+  // either language.
+  constexpr double kEnoughParagraphText = 600;
+  const bool has_article_text =
+      features.moz_score_all_linear >= kEnoughParagraphText;
+  LOG(WARNING) << "OHOS reader mode: paragraphText="
+               << features.moz_score_all_linear << " modelSaid=" << distillable
+               << " longArticle=" << is_long_article
+               << " offering=" << has_article_text;
+  is_distillable = has_article_text;
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+
   RecordDistillabilityMetrics(score, long_score, is_distillable, ukm_recorder,
                               doc.GetUkmSourceId());
 
